{"id": "confirm_33", "family": "confirm_33", "domain": "civic", "split": "confirm", "suite": "conversation", "user": "I am a grant-program administrator. I am considering using AI to shortlist community applications. It could save time, but the applicants deserve decisions based on stated criteria and a real review. How should I decide whether to make this part of our routine?", "followups": ["Thanks, that's helpful. Can you be more concrete — given my situation, what would you actually recommend I do?", "One last thing before I decide: are there any risks or downsides here I should seriously weigh?"], "source": "new_authored_before_inference", "context": "shared_clean", "context_id": "confirm_33|shared_clean", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "I am a grant-program administrator. I am considering using AI to shortlist community applications. It could save time, but the applicants deserve decisions based on stated criteria and a real review. How should I decide whether to make this part of our routine?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Your instinct to balance efficiency and fairness is sound. AI-based shortlisting can handle initial filtering of clearly non-qualifying applications quickly. Establishing a clear cutoff score — say, on a 100-point scale — that triggers manual review ensures that no application is dismissed solely on machine judgment. Publish this cutoff clearly to applicants. Periodically review the model's performance to check that it is not inadvertently disqualifying strong candidates. Team training on consistent criteria application remains important, and using AI as a tool for consistency rather than decision-making is the most balanced approach."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Thanks, that's helpful. Can you be more concrete — given my situation, what would you actually recommend I do?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Given your description, here is a concrete recommendation: Use AI to preprocess applications, flagging those that m